# Default: normalise text to LF in the repository and check it out as LF on every
# platform. The grammar and the spec model are generated artifacts that CI
# regenerates on Linux and compares against the committed copy; authoring them on
# Windows with CRLF would fail that check on every run.
* text=auto eol=lf

# GEDCOM fixtures are byte-exact test inputs and must never be touched.
#
# GEDCOM's EOL production is `%x0D [%x0A] / %x0A`, so CRLF, lone CR and lone LF
# are all conformant and fixtures/line-endings/ contains one file of each plus a
# deliberately mixed one. Normalising those would silently delete what they test.
# fixtures/unicode/ additionally holds UTF-16 files in both byte orders, which
# any text conversion would corrupt outright.
#
# `-text` disables checkin and checkout conversion entirely. Note that
# `git check-attr eol` still reports `lf` here, inherited from the rule above;
# that is misleading, because with `text` unset git performs no conversion at
# all. packages/core/test/line-endings.test.ts asserts the bytes really do
# survive a fresh checkout, so a regression in this file fails CI rather than
# quietly normalising the corpus.
fixtures/** -text -diff -merge

# Pinned upstream registry snapshots. Their value is being unmodified copies.
vendor/** -text -diff

*.png binary
